jit++ is a cooperative tracing jit written in c++ that compiles x86-64 assembly into x86-64 assembly using user placed callsites as monitored hotspots.

(c) 2008 Edward Kmett
All Rights Reserved

Third-Party Dependencies:

udis86				-- use for debugging assembly 
google logging framework (glog) -- used to show output
google flags (gflags)           -- used to pass options to the tracer
libtool				-- used by the build process
    boost unit test framework 1.36	-- used by the test suite

Resurrection baseline (Linux x86-64)
===================================

The original interpreter and assembly entry/exit trampoline build with modern
GCC. This is an experimental interpreter foundation, not a completed optimizing
JIT. Unsupported instructions return to native execution at the original PC;
modern libc commonly triggers this path on AVX instructions.

On Debian/Ubuntu, install the build prerequisites:

    sudo apt-get install build-essential autoconf automake libtool git python3 \
        libgoogle-glog-dev libgflags-dev

udis86 is used for diagnostic disassembly. If it is not already installed, the
helper builds a pinned upstream revision locally, fixing its Python 3 integer
division compatibility:

    sh bin/build-udis86.sh "$PWD/build-deps"
    export CPPFLAGS="-I$PWD/build-deps/include"
    export LDFLAGS="-L$PWD/build-deps/lib -Wl,-rpath,$PWD/build-deps/lib"

Bootstrap, build, and run the tests:

    sh bin/autogen.sh

Or run the stages individually:

    autoreconf -fi
    ./configure
    make -j2
    make check

The active tests do not require Boost. test_interpreter runs the original demo;
test_roundtrip uses a deterministic assembly sequence to check interpreted
arithmetic, stores, arithmetic flags on native resumption, repeated entry,
step-limited exit, and unsupported-instruction fallback. The assertions remain
active in optimized builds. For instruction logging:

    ./test_interpreter --logtostderr --v=1

The original design still uses a worker pthread and a fixed gap on the caller's
stack. Instruction coverage, syscall/TLS behavior, and extended vector state are
incomplete; the round-trip tests establish a baseline, not general x86-64
correctness. The old Boost tests remain disabled.

Existing Autotools deprecation warnings and return-type warnings in the legacy
flags helpers remain; those paths need further correctness work.
